 movement that have sort of practical  guidelines for research teams one from  the national research council and one  from the national cancer institute  both of those are available freely  online  so my role in most of my projects has  been as the social scientist on a team  of scientists and engineers  and that is not enough  that's an important first step a lot of  funders now require a social scientist  or an ethicist to be on these large  collaborative teams  but let me tell you it's not enough one  person alone can't ensure ethical  collaboration it's too it's too hard  it's too complex  and so i would argue that instead of  having one facilitator i would train  everybody in facilitation skills okay  there's lots of webinars workshops where  you can learn how to work well with  others  rather than outsourcing it to somebody  else it's better if we all do it  together we all have skin in the game  and then finally most importantly we  need to practice  trust respect and listening actually do  it not just talk about it so here are  some ways we can do that  and one is to write a memorandum of  understanding  um this lots of teams do this sometimes  it's pretty formalized and it's deciding  things like authorship and data sharing  who is it who is the leader who is  responsible for mentoring how do we  handle complaints  um so lots of teams do this already  and we should all do it and we should  follow it and we should remember that  it's a living document that should be  updated and we should it should be a  starting point for building consensus  um throughout the life of the project  so you can google around and find  examples for these in some templates  there's something called the contributor  roles taxonomy  that i think is really helpful that lays  out all the kinds of work that are part  of scientific research and then helps  you decide  how to label a contributor so beyond my  list of you know technicians and  students and volunteers  um it breaks it down more into like data  analysts versus data creators versus  data interpreters okay and then that can  help you figure out how people in your  team fit together  um  this should be obvious but we should  respect everyone's contributions with  pay  okay i talked about volunteers a little  bit and students who sometimes work  without pay  we should pay everybody for their time  and intellectual property okay so  everybody's making an epistemic  contribution  that should be recognized and that's  going to be different in different  fields and different contributors are  going to want different kinds of  recognition so that's gonna be a  discussion among your teammates how do  how do people want to be recognized for  their contributions both of labor and of  knowledge  and there are two documents that are  based on each other and they're lovely  called the collaborators bill of light  bill of rights these are written by  scholars and digital humanities and it's  really about defining the roles of  collaborators and particularly of  student collaborators to how to and how  to avoid  exploitation  of various kinds of workers  and then of course inviting and  responding to feedback creating spaces  for open communication  is a crucial part of team science and  partly this is to inspire reflection  just like an undergrad asking a question  is going to make a grad student stop and  think through what they're doing and  that's good for the science  and  if we invite reflection among our team  members then we're all going to stop  what we're doing and make sure that what  we're doing fits what we actually want  to be doing what we should be doing  ethically and morally and epistemically  thanks  kaelin that was fantastic and it gives  us so much more to think about in  broadening  the  ethics of  biosciences so much more broadly as we  you know you talked about working with  uh you know engineering teams going to  remote locations and working with you  know local communities and indigenous  communities where and you mentioned  trust  which i think is just so important  because you know so many of these  communities have just been they've been  exploited they you know this is like  okay now there's another group of  probably predominantly white people  going to come in and tell us how you  know we should be living our lives and  they just after the umpteenth time of  this possibly over you know decades and  decades you know they're not going to be  very trustful and their people are going  to come in and take their collect their  data points and go away and never see  the results of that again so i think  that's so important and it's it's  important not only for  dealing with  indigenous peoples um you know wherever  they are alaska or or hawaii or here in  north america but also in some of our  biosample um data  communities that we go and get data from  so people with autism spectrum disorder  people with mental health disorders  people with you know other neurological  um disorders or other things we we tend  to you know they're they're sort of a  means to an end right they're going to  get us the data point we want we're  going to go ahead and write great papers  um so i think that's just so was such an  important statement and i'm  curious about your own experiences with  this what you have seen  in terms of the positive feedback you've  possibly gotten from you know the native  alaskans uh in your own work  yeah thanks i really didn't even talk  about research participants and and  indigenous  data sets are an amazing example of  potential exploitation and potential  mutual benefit  right if those data are used  appropriately respectfully  there can be a lot of good that can come  out of biomedical collaborations with  indigenous communities and kim talber is  a scholar in my field who writes about  indigenous data rights over biosamples  and it's she her arguments are fabulous  on how to do that well  and so for us we're still sort of  figuring out  how to best serve our partners in new  kayak vic and we've i told you some  examples of how they've already sort of  corrected our course thank goodness  and another project i did with  charlottesville residents is a little  maybe a better example of that which is  advocates in charlottesville are pushing  the city to build an open data portal to  share the city's data online publicly  and so i was working with them to try to  figure out what that portal should look  like and what should be on it and who  should be in charge of it  and and we came up with really different  goals between what the city wanted and  what the citizens wanted  and what people who didn't know anything  about data wanted which was me right i  have no idea how to use open data sets  and  except that i knew that they were a good  thing and so how do we make this  this object this portal work for the  city with its concerns about privacy  and its concerns about democratic  engagement right open data can be a way  to pull people in and help people  develop an argument for the need for  change and the data advocates who just  wanted all the data and wanted it now  and the rest of us who would like to  learn what we can learn from using these  kinds of projects so yeah bringing  together all those different  stakeholders  um is crucial for the success of the  research and also crucial for the  success of addressing real social  problems like infrastructure like  democratic democratic engagement like  government transparency  yeah i like that and i was also struck  by  your comments about um  inclusion of say undergraduates in the  research enterprise  and  i'm  old enough now myself to have seen a lot  of people who are older than me simply  just flat out refuse to include  undergraduates as part of you know on an  author list just you know oh they're  undergraduates you know what do they  know they were just the pair of helping  hands and so we don't need to give them  any sort of  authorship credit  and in my own life i've tried to do that  where possible because like you know  what's the harm right it's fact it's  good right it's good for that student it  like helps them and you know one of the  things you know as somebody who's sat on  a lot of graduate um uh student you know  uh admissions committees is that one of  the things we look for is paging through  their applications say how many papers  have they published and if it's you know  oh they're not supposed to or no we  don't allow them you're denying them  this wonderful you know ticket to the  research you know  ticket to entry into the research world  that uh had you just given them the  authorship it probably would have gotten  them past the door so i don't know i i'm  i'm intrigued by that and um i'm curious  if you've got any examples yourself or  you know of anybody who for example they  were included on some early paper and  now they're like you know  i don't know they're  national you know they've got some big  award or they're now you know some  superstar who had that opportunity i  don't know if that makes any sense but  uh yeah yeah no absolutely i think  that's another argument for equity and  inclusion  that if students don't have papers  they're not going to get into grad  school in engineering at least like  you're expected to have a paper when you  graduate undergrad and that's  you have to communicate that expectation  to the pi so that they'll put the  undergrads on and so that they'll  rethink that assumption about  oh undergrads don't deserve to be  authors you know or they'll plan  i've done this myself where i plan  papers that we're writing really for the  benefit of the undergrads like it's  preliminary results for example like  we're not going to finish the study  while that undergrad is a student so  let's write up something now so that  that student can be on it  and then we'll build on that paper in  the future and that's been great for me  right it forces me to get some papers  out the door  um and then that student has  proof of the like incredible  contributions that they've made and i  think a lot of people  you know i'm being a little facetious  because of course undergrads need a lot  of training just like anybody to do  research well  um but one of the most valuable things i  found about working with undergrads  is that they show up  you know every tuesday at one i'm going  to have a student in my office who's  going to look at me really eagerly and  say what am i doing this week that's  right which keeps me on target and make  sure that i've done my part so that i  don't disappoint the student and that  has actually been an incredible  boon to my own research just like the  relentlessness of it that you you want  the undergrads depending on you and so  therefore you produce  and i think only undergrads could do  that if it were a collaborator i think  we wouldn't be as  as quick you know we wouldn't we don't  meet every week with our collaborators  because we're off doing our own things  and so undergrads can provide that sort  of  pressure slash support to keep going  so absolutely absolutely and  undergraduates i've had in my labs in  various institutions um i've always  tried to give them those opportunities  either to you know present a poster at a  big conference or to you know have first  author publications on things that you  know what's the harm it's no harm to me  i'm still an author right i'm just the  you know the guy at the end of the list  right but the per the people who are  actually doing the work you know give  them that opportunity and i had one  student who i just thought the world of  um and i always tried to make sure that  she had opportunities to lead  you know the writing of papers and  whatnot um at the this was while she was  kind of an undergraduate and kind of  after she had graduated but she was  still working in the lab um  she ended up with enough  peer-reviewed publications  of herself that she was on  uh that her age index was like higher  than some of the junior faculty that we  were considering  and she's now a grad she eventually went  to grad graduate school herself and now  she's a graduate student but i always  thought you know gosh if we just were  able to do that for so many more  students that would be wonderful  right and also as a recognition of the  work that they've done yeah just to help  their career but to like say thank you  you know they earned that  absolutely and so  one of the things about the i guess  bringing this back to ethical ai is is  as we're thinking about you know we  collect this data and we use it to build  models and then presumably we want to  make predictions about like i don't know  where permafrost is melting more quickly  or what what have you and you're using  computational models on that how do some  of these ethical  considerations map themselves if they do  it all into you know  turning these things into code or into  mathematical models do you have any  sense about that  yeah i do and i think ethically ai is  is scary because we think it's so  technical we got so so focused on the  math and the coding and the models and  the predictive aspects and  it's easy to just say just build the  thing and we'll figure out who's  responsible for it later we'll figure  out what your contribution was later you  know we'll figure out how we're going to  share this later and that's really  dangerous that's a way to exploit  particularly junior research members  and  you know underrepresented minorities are  going to bear the brunt of that  discrimination  and so it worries me about ethical ai  that  it's it's so people think of it as being  so technical and it's not like it's  still a team of people working together  to build this thing and so we need to  respect that team  as a sort of living breathing organism  that we need to sustain and support and  create governance structure for  and provide training and mentor you know  what i mean like all these things there  has to be so much learning  on a team of people working on ethical  ai because it relies on so many  disciplines and so many kinds of  knowledge  um  so yeah i gave this talk bringing  together a wide variety of fields on  purpose to make the point that i think  these these recommendations i'm making  are true for any field of research or  design  well you've certainly touched on one of  the main thrusts of our biomedical data  science innovation lab is to bring these  different voices together with different  points of view and different  perspectives different backgrounds  different kind of  sets of you know skill sets that they  can bring together because you know so  often  how many times have we seen something  where i don't know a bunch of people  just cut from the same cloth get  together and do something and then say  hey everybody look what we built you  should all want to use this or whatever  it is and peop people who are not within  that you mentioned kind of the outsiders  right we'll take a look at it and go i  would never have done it that way and  what you've created is completely  useless to me if you had my input i  might have been able to give you some  different you know more nuanced  understanding of this area and so anyway  bringing all these different voices  together is is  such an important element of of research  of ethical ai and certainly part of our  biomedical data science innovation  i'm so glad  yes  well um  i think we're  about done caitlin wiley from university  of virginia thank you so much for  sharing your uh insights and wisdom and  uh ideas about team science and  collaboration and what all that means  for ethical ai we really appreciate it  thank you thank you everybody for for  joining us and uh
 

